# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "Swapping two variables", question.
id: swap-no-temp
title: Swapping without a temporary variable
topic: Sequential composition
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "Swapping two variables", question
tags: assignment, sequence, wp, counterexample

task:
  We try to swap $x$ and $y$ without a temporary variable, with
  `x := y; y := x`. $A$ and $B$ are logical variables: the program does
  not change them, and they name the initial values of $x$ and $y$.

  Calculate the weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition
  $Q$, statement by statement from the last one. Then use the consequence
  rule to decide whether the triple is valid. For which values of $A$ and
  $B$ does the program still work?

pre: x = A && y = B
post: x = B && y = A

program:
  {P}
  {R2}
  x := y;      // i1
  {R1}
  y := x       // i2
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of y := x
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  The precondition of an assignment is obtained by substituting the
  assigned variable ($y$) with the assignment expression ($x$) in the
  postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(y := x, Q)
  = (x = B && y = A)[y <- x]
  = x = B && x = A        // y is replaced by x; x = B has no y
set: R1 = x = B && x = A

step: R2, the weakest precondition of x := y
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment expression
  ($y$) in the postcondition of this statement, which is $R1$.
calc:
  wp(x := y, R1)
  = (x = B && x = A)[x <- y]
  = y = B && y = A        // both x are replaced by y
set: R2 = y = B && y = A

step: The precondition must imply the weakest precondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE
text:
  $R2$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R2$.
vc: x = A && y = B ==> y = B && y = A
status: fails
counterexample: x = 1, y = 2, A = 1, B = 2
why:
  $P$ gives $y = B$, but $y = A$ also needs $A = B$. With $A = 1$ and
  $B = 2$ the implication is false. From $x = 1$, $y = 2$ the program
  first sets $x$ to $2$, then $y$ to $2$. The initial value $1$ of $x$ is
  lost, so $y = A$ is false at the end.
result: The triple is not valid. The program works only when $A = B$, that is, when $x$ and $y$ start with the same value.

summary:
  $R2$ says that $y$ is equal to both $A$ and $B$. $P$ implies it only
  when $A = B$. The first assignment overwrites $x$ before its value is
  copied, which is why the temporary variable is needed.
